<section id="ancient"><div class="wrap">
<h2>Level 1: Ancient games</h2>
<p class="lead">Many of these games trained the mind for strategy, patience and leadership. Several spread across the world and became games we play today.</p>
<figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 800 230" role="img" aria-label="Illustration of the four divisions of the ancient Indian army"><rect width="800" height="230" fill="#0b0b0b"/><line x1="200" y1="20" x2="200" y2="190" stroke="#2c2c2c"/><line x1="400" y1="20" x2="400" y2="190" stroke="#2c2c2c"/><line x1="600" y1="20" x2="600" y2="190" stroke="#2c2c2c"/><circle cx="100" cy="70" r="16" fill="#f2f2f2"/><rect x="86" y="90" width="28" height="52" rx="8" fill="#f2f2f2"/><path d="M92 142 L88 172 M108 142 L112 172" stroke="#f2f2f2" stroke-width="8" stroke-linecap="round"/><circle cx="80" cy="115" r="17" fill="#e63946" stroke="#f2f2f2" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M132 40 L132 176" stroke="#f2f2f2" stroke-width="4"/><path d="M132 28 L124 50 L140 50 Z" fill="#e63946"/><ellipse cx="300" cy="118" rx="46" ry="24" fill="#f2f2f2"/><path d="M328 108 L352 56 L374 52 L388 68 L372 74 L364 72 L350 104 Z" fill="#f2f2f2"/><path d="M354 54 L358 40 L366 52 Z" fill="#f2f2f2"/><path d="M270 136 L266 172 M286 140 L284 172 M316 140 L318 172 M332 136 L338 172" stroke="#f2f2f2" stroke-width="7" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M256 112 Q232 126 244 162" stroke="#f2f2f2" stroke-width="6" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/><rect x="288" y="94" width="28" height="12" rx="3" fill="#e63946"/><circle cx="372" cy="64" r="2.5" fill="#000"/><ellipse cx="500" cy="118" rx="52" ry="34" fill="#f2f2f2"/><circle cx="552" cy="106" r="24" fill="#f2f2f2"/><ellipse cx="540" cy="108" rx="11" ry="18" fill="#bdbdbd"/><path d="M566 116 Q590 150 562 166 Q550 170 552 158" stroke="#f2f2f2" stroke-width="11" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M562 120 Q584 122 592 110" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="4" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/><rect x="466" y="140" width="13" height="34" rx="4" fill="#f2f2f2"/><rect x="486" y="144" width="13" height="30" rx="4" fill="#f2f2f2"/><rect x="516" y="144" width="13" height="30" rx="4" fill="#f2f2f2"/><rect x="536" y="140" width="13" height="34" rx="4" fill="#f2f2f2"/><rect x="478" y="76" width="46" height="20" fill="#e63946"/><path d="M474 76 L501 54 L528 76 Z" fill="#e63946"/><circle cx="556" cy="100" r="2.5" fill="#000"/><circle cx="690" cy="158" r="28" fill="none" stroke="#f2f2f2" stroke-width="6"/><path d="M690 130 V186 M662 158 H718 M670 138 L710 178 M710 138 L670 178" stroke="#f2f2f2" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="654" y="96" width="70" height="40" rx="4" fill="#e63946"/><path d="M724 118 L772 108" stroke="#f2f2f2" stroke-width="5"/><path d="M664 96 V56" stroke="#f2f2f2" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M664 56 L688 64 L664 74 Z" fill="#f2f2f2"/><text x="100" y="208" font-family="Hind,sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#f2f2f2" text-anchor="middle">Padati · Infantry</text><text x="300" y="208" font-family="Hind,sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#f2f2f2" text-anchor="middle">Ashva · Cavalry</text><text x="500" y="208" font-family="Hind,sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#f2f2f2" text-anchor="middle">Gaja · Elephants</text><text x="700" y="208" font-family="Hind,sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#f2f2f2" text-anchor="middle">Ratha · Chariots</text></svg><figcaption>The four divisions of the <i>Chaturangini Sena</i> (illustration). They became the pawn, knight, bishop and rook of chess.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Chaturanga</h3><p>Developed in the Gupta period (around the 6th century CE). Its four divisions are the <i>Chaturangini Sena</i> of the epics: infantry, cavalry, elephants and chariots. It is the ancestor of modern chess.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Pachisi / Chausar</h3><p>A cross-shaped dice and race game, played in royal courts. The dice game of the Mahabharata is linked to it. It led to Ludo.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Moksha Patam</h3><p>A moral game where virtues move you up and vices drag you down. It became Snakes and Ladders.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Kabaddi</h3><p>A team contact sport of raiding and defending. It mirrors the Chakravyuha story and is now a professional league.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Mallakhamba</h3><p>Gymnastics on a wooden pole, built for body control and balance, as used in warrior training.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Gilli-danda</h3><p>A stick and tip-cat game that builds aim and timing, an ancestor of bat-and-ball games.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Kalaripayattu</h3><p>A martial art from Kerala, traditionally taught with body training, sticks, swords and shields. It is counted among the oldest fighting systems in the world.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Silambam</h3><p>A staff-fighting art from Tamil Nadu that trains footwork, reach and defence. Like a game class, each weapon has its own skill.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Kho-kho</h3><p>A chase game of team tactics, speed and sudden direction changes. It is now played in national and international tournaments.</p></div>
