<section id="dive" style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap">
<h2>Deep dives: stories behind the games</h2>
<p class="lead">Open each card to read the stories and facts that connect ancient Indian knowledge with the games we play today.</p>
<details open><summary>Why is the bishop an elephant?</summary><p>In Chaturanga the <i>Gaja</i> (elephant) was one of the four army divisions. When the game travelled to the Arab world, the piece was called <i>al-fil</i>, "the elephant", and the Spanish word <i>alfil</i> for the bishop still comes from it. Russian chess players still call the bishop <i>slon</i>, which means elephant. Western Europeans later reimagined the piece as a bishop, but its Indian origin stayed hidden in these names.</p></details>
<details><summary>The thirteenth day: Abhimanyu and the Chakravyuha</summary><p>In the Drona Parva of the Mahabharata, Dronacharya forms the Chakravyuha on the thirteenth day of the Kurukshetra war. Only Arjuna and Krishna know how to break it, and both are drawn away. Arjuna's young son Abhimanyu volunteers to enter. Tradition says he heard the secret of entering while still in his mother Subhadra's womb, but never heard how to leave. He fights bravely but is surrounded and killed by several warriors at once, which breaks the rules of dharma-yuddha. This is why the Chakravyuha is remembered as a story about knowledge, courage and the cost of incomplete learning.</p></details>
<details><summary>Chanakya and the book that disappeared</summary><p>The Arthashastra is attributed to Kautilya (also called Chanakya), the adviser of Chandragupta Maurya. It has 15 books covering the economy, law, spies, diplomacy and war. For centuries the text was thought lost, until a manuscript was found in the early 1900s and translated by R. Shamasastry. Today its ideas on alliances and power are studied in political science and strategy courses, and some scholars compare them to game theory.</p></details>
<details><summary>The wheat grains puzzle</summary><p>A famous legend says the inventor of chess asked a king for one grain of wheat on the first square, two on the second, four on the third, and so on, doubling on each of the 64 squares. The total is 18,446,744,073,709,551,615 grains, far more wheat than has ever been grown. The story is a lesson in exponential growth, and it is often told in India as part of the history of chess and mathematics.</p></details>
<details><summary>Dice, dharma and the cost of gambling</summary><p>In the Mahabharata, Yudhishthira loses his wealth, his kingdom, his brothers and finally Draupadi in a game of dice, and the Pandavas are sent into exile. Indian texts treat gambling as a danger to families and kingdoms, and the Arthashastra describes state regulation of gambling. This is a useful ethical link to today's games: the same old question of play versus addiction appears in debates about loot boxes and excessive screen time.</p></details>
<details><summary>Kabaddi: from villages to a league</summary><p>Kabaddi is an ancient team sport that tradition links to the raid and defence tactics of warriors, and the Abhimanyu story is often used to explain it. It became an Asian Games sport in 1990, and the Pro Kabaddi League began in 2014 and brought the game to a huge television audience. Each raid is like a small military operation: a raider enters enemy territory, tags defenders and must return before the breath of the chant runs out.</p></details>
<details><summary>Temple walls, paintings and Raji</summary><p>Indian temples told the epics through stone carvings and wall paintings long before screens existed. The developers of Raji say they use carvings and paintings in the environment to tell mythical stories, and the game's look is based on Pahari painting and medieval Rajasthani forts. In this way, a modern video game continues an old tradition: using art and play to carry a culture's stories to a new generation.</p></details>
</div></section>

